{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of exact pushed head 83a3f1d2cc6dec7367a10f97ee98ec8953976aea on request/quarantine-git-config, for review request c8a7bf79 under promise 36b1bcf9, naming artifact edecb4dc at internal/gitstore. Governing implementation request 1e0644d1.\n\nDISCLOSURE FIRST. I raised this defect myself in triage batch one (721531a1), so I have a stake in the framing. I therefore judged the head against the code and against request 1e0644d1's own conditions, not against my description of the problem, and I looked specifically for ways my framing might have been too narrow. One such gap is recorded below as a stated limit rather than buried.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layer is internal/gitstore, the Git store. Its contract is preserved and no exported surface moves: the four new functions — runHermetic, runWithEnvironment, hermeticGitEnvironment and trustedSSHKeygen — are all unexported, Store.run keeps its exact signature and behaviour for every existing caller, and no other package changes. docs/reference/architecture.md therefore needs no update in this head, and an update would have been the finding. The refactor is the right shape: runWithEnvironment holds the one exec site, and run and runHermetic differ only in which environment they hand it, so there is a single place where a future caller chooses a posture.\n\nSECURITY. The defect 1e0644d1 names is closed at the boundary it names. I verified the three vectors independently rather than reading the report.\n\nInherited ambient configuration. hermeticGitEnvironment strips GIT_CONFIG and every GIT_CONFIG_-prefixed variable from the inherited environment before appending GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null and GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null. The strip is what makes this sound rather than merely conventional: GIT_CONFIG_COUNT, GIT_CONFIG_KEY_n and GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_n all carry that prefix, so the command-scoped injection channel is removed rather than overridden, and the three appended values cannot be shadowed by an earlier entry the attacker set.\n\nRepository-local configuration. This is the vector the environment variables do not reach, because the head deliberately keeps .git/config readable so the repository can still declare its object format. It is closed instead by pinning gpg.ssh.program on the command line at gitstore.go:317-318 and :647-649, which outranks repository config in Git's precedence order.\n\nCoverage of both signing paths. commit-tree -S and verify-commit are the only two sites in the tree that sign or verify, and both now go through runHermetic with the pin. There is no third path to miss.\n\nThe regression is real, not decorative. TestSigningAndVerificationQuarantineExecutableGitConfig exercises all three vectors at once — a repository-local gpg.ssh.program, a malformed hostile GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, and GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY_0/VALUE_0 — and asserts both that signing and verification still succeed and that the sentinel marker file never appears. I mutation-checked it rather than trusting green: five separate mutations, each applied alone in a disposable worktree and reverted afterwards. Reverting signing to the non-hermetic run, reverting verification to the non-hermetic run, dropping the signing pin, dropping the verification pin, and disabling the GIT_CONFIG_ strip loop each turn the test red. Every part of the guard is pinned by something.\n\nONE STATED LIMIT, WHICH I AM NOT REQUESTING CHANGES FOR. trustedSSHKeygen resolves the program with exec.LookPath, which searches the inherited PATH, and hermeticGitEnvironment deliberately preserves PATH. So the executable is pinned against configuration injection but not against PATH: an attacker who controls PATH still selects the binary, and filepath.Abs then hands Git an absolute path to the attacker's choice. This is not a defect in the head, for two reasons. First, request 1e0644d1 asks that hostile repository and inherited configuration cannot invoke a sentinel program, and that is exactly what is achieved. Second, anyone who can set PATH for the resident process is already inside the trusted-process boundary, which service.TrustedProcessPosture and SECURITY.md say plainly is not defended. But the artifact text reads \"pins the resolved SSH signing executable\", which a later reader could take as PATH protection, so I am recording the true scope here where the verdict will be read alongside it. If PATH-independence is ever wanted, that is a fresh request about the deployment posture, not a change to this head.\n\nRelated and out of scope, recorded so it is not mistaken for an oversight: GenerateSSHKey at gitstore.go:680 still invokes bare \"ssh-keygen\" without trustedSSHKeygen. That is an inconsistency of style rather than of strength, because both forms resolve through PATH and neither is more trusted than the other, and key generation is not a Git command and so is not within 1e0644d1's condition. The five other direct git invocations, at gitstore.go:53, :361, :460 and :571 and cmd/gs/main.go:1873, remain non-hermetic; none signs or verifies, and I could not construct an execution vector through any of them, so they are outside this request. My other batch-one finding, 9e337057 on replacement objects, is untouched here and correctly so — it is a separate request.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. I looked for concrete removable complexity and found nothing worth cutting that does not weaken 1e0644d1. Two candidates, both examined and both rejected. GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null is strictly redundant with GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1 on any Git that honours the newer variable, but the pair costs one line, and the older variable is universal while the newer is not, so keeping both is defence that survives a downgrade rather than fluff. hermeticGitEnvironment builds append(os.Environ(), extra...) inside the range clause after already sizing the slice from len(os.Environ()), which copies the environment twice; that is a negligible allocation on a path that then forks a process, and collapsing it would trade a clear loop for a fiddly one. Neither is worth a round trip. The change is 55 added lines in the implementation for three closed vectors, which is proportionate.\n\nGATES. Re-run by me at the exact head, not trusted. go vet ./... exit 0. go test -race ./internal/gitstore -count=1 ok in 6.007s. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with all 27 packages ok, including cmd/gs in 212.8s, internal/kernel in 204.9s and internal/docset in 123.1s. The head fast-forwards from main 92125957 and the diff is the two files claimed, +101/-3. Artifact edecb4dc is live, unretired, standing at this exact commit, with exactly one live predecessor, 7c05c81c at a81d9c4a, so the merge receipt has a single clean predecessor to retire at internal/gitstore.\n\nAny change to this head invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:edecb4dc638eb6e1ee73276f73008844d16b6223","head":"83a3f1d2cc6dec7367a10f97ee98ec8953976aea","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:23c336b4c36d39576422c1f2ec6171cb1810679f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:439ff2b9a41801b43261065ccf7a6b6c26af1196, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:47cdbf121120aa8704e22a464e6abdb040439429, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b463eb70ab2307bc417fd8a3ae04e8be6f2bfba3 and 2 more","verdict":"approved"}}